import json
units=[]
def add(id,title,desc,icon,group,parts,trap,example,model):
 units.append(dict(id=id,title=title,desc=desc,icon=icon,group=group,parts=parts,trap=trap,example=example,model=model))
add('water','Water & its superpowers','How a tiny uneven molecule makes life possible.','◉','Unit 2',[
['Start with the uneven pull','A water molecule has two hydrogen atoms joined to one oxygen atom by polar covalent bonds. Oxygen pulls shared electrons more strongly, so its end is slightly negative (δ−). The hydrogen ends are slightly positive (δ+). These are partial charges: the whole molecule is neutral.'],
['From polarity to hydrogen bonds','The slightly positive hydrogen of one water molecule attracts the slightly negative oxygen of another. That attraction is a hydrogen bond. Individual hydrogen bonds are weaker than covalent bonds, but many together have a big effect.'],
['Cohesion, adhesion, and surface tension','Cohesion means water sticks to water. Adhesion means water sticks to other polar surfaces, such as the walls of narrow tubes in plants. Together these help water move through narrow spaces. Surface tension comes from cohesion at the surface.'],
['A stable home for life','Water absorbs a lot of energy before its temperature changes much. This helps bodies and lakes resist rapid temperature swings. Evaporation removes higher-energy molecules and cools a surface, which is why sweating works.'],
['Dissolving and floating','Water surrounds many ions and polar molecules, allowing them to dissolve. Nonpolar oils do not mix well with water. In ice, hydrogen bonds hold molecules in an open arrangement, making ice less dense than liquid water. Floating ice can insulate water below.']],
'Water is not a universal solvent that dissolves everything. Also, the O–H bonds inside a water molecule are covalent; hydrogen bonds mostly connect different molecules.',
'A drop clings to a leaf: adhesion helps it stick to the leaf, and cohesion helps the drop stay together.','water')
add('ph','pH & chemical clues','Understand acids, bases, buffers, and lab indicators.','⌁','Unit 2',[
['What the number means','pH describes hydrogen ion concentration. At about 25°C, pH below 7 is acidic, 7 is neutral, and above 7 is basic. Acids increase H⁺ in water; bases reduce H⁺ concentration, often by accepting H⁺ or releasing OH⁻.'],
['Each step is tenfold','pH 3 has ten times the hydrogen ion concentration of pH 4, and 100 times that of pH 5. A lower pH means more H⁺. A two-unit change is not just twice as much!'],
['Why cells care','Proteins work best in particular chemical conditions. A large pH change can alter interactions that hold a protein in shape. Buffers resist pH changes by accepting or releasing H⁺. They do not make every solution neutral.'],
['Use evidence, not guesses','An indicator changes color under particular conditions. Litmus tests acidity or basicity; phenolphthalein is pink in sufficiently basic solutions. Iodine turns blue-black with starch. Heated Benedict’s solution detects reducing sugars such as glucose. Bromothymol blue responds to pH; dissolved CO₂ can lower pH, shifting its color toward yellow.']],
'Bromothymol blue responds directly to pH, not uniquely to CO₂. A color result supports a conclusion only when you know the test conditions and use controls.',
'Compare an unknown food with a glucose positive control and water negative control. Heat all Benedict’s samples the same way before comparing colors.','ph')
add('building','Building & breaking molecules','Monomers, polymers, and the role of water.','⊕','Unit 2',[
['Small pieces, larger structures','A monomer is a small building block. A polymer is a chain of repeating building blocks. Monosaccharides build polysaccharides; amino acids build polypeptides; nucleotides build DNA and RNA. Lipids are grouped with biological macromolecules, but are not true repeating-unit polymers.'],
['Dehydration synthesis: build','In a simplified condensation reaction, an H from one building block and an OH from another form water. A covalent bond links the building blocks. Think: joining pieces releases water. Cells use enzymes and energy-coupled pathways to assemble molecules.'],
['Hydrolysis: break','Hydro means water; lysis means splitting. Hydrolysis uses water to break a covalent bond, adding H and OH to the resulting pieces. Digestion uses enzyme-catalyzed hydrolysis to turn large food molecules into smaller absorbable molecules.'],
['Count connections','A single unbranched chain of n monomers has n−1 connections between monomers. In the simplified dehydration model, joining five separate monomers into one chain releases four water molecules. Breaking all four connections uses four waters.']],
'Water is released during dehydration synthesis and used during hydrolysis. Breaking a bond itself requires energy; do not assume every breakdown reaction automatically releases usable energy.',
'A starch chain is too large to absorb directly. Digestive enzymes help hydrolyze it into smaller sugars that can be absorbed.','building')
add('molecules','The four molecule families','Carbohydrates, lipids, proteins, and nucleic acids.','⬡','Unit 2',[
['Carbohydrates: fuel and framework','Simple sugars such as glucose are monosaccharides. Two linked sugars make a disaccharide; many make a polysaccharide. Starch stores glucose in plants, glycogen stores it in animals, and cellulose supports plant cell walls. Many carbohydrates have an approximate 1:2:1 C:H:O ratio, but it is not a universal identification rule.'],
['Lipids: storage and membranes','Fats store lots of energy, help insulate, and cushion organs. A triglyceride contains glycerol and three fatty acids. Phospholipids form the basic structure of cell membranes. Steroids are also lipids. Lipids are largely nonpolar and hydrophobic.'],
['Saturated or unsaturated?','Saturated fatty acid chains have no carbon–carbon double bonds. Cis unsaturated chains have one or more double bonds that create kinks, making tight packing harder. This often makes fats more fluid. Trans double bonds are straighter than cis ones. Partial hydrogenation can create trans fats; adding hydrogen is not the definition of a trans bond.'],
['Proteins: the cell’s workers','Amino acids link into polypeptides that fold into functional proteins. Proteins act as enzymes, transporters, receptors, antibodies, and structural supports. Each amino acid has an amino group, a carboxyl group, a hydrogen, and an R group attached to its central carbon. R groups differ.'],
['Nucleic acids: information','DNA and RNA are polymers of nucleotides. Each nucleotide has a sugar, a phosphate group, and a nitrogen-containing base. DNA stores hereditary information; RNA helps express that information, including during protein production. Food is not the only place these molecules exist: every living cell contains biological molecules.']],
'Carbohydrates do more than provide energy, and proteins do more than build muscles. Identify a molecule using its structure and function together.',
'A potato stores starch. Your liver stores glycogen. A plant cell wall contains cellulose. All three are carbohydrates, but their structures and jobs differ.','families')
add('proteins','Protein shape → protein job','Follow a chain from amino acids to a working protein.','〰','Unit 2',[
['Primary: the sequence','Primary structure is the order of amino acids in a polypeptide. Covalent peptide bonds join the carboxyl carbon of one amino acid to the amino nitrogen of another. A sequence change can change folding, but does not always change function.'],
['Secondary: local patterns','Some backbone regions form alpha helices or beta sheets. Hydrogen bonds between backbone C=O and N–H groups stabilize these patterns. These are backbone interactions, not the R-group interactions that define tertiary folding.'],
['Tertiary: one chain’s overall fold','A polypeptide folds into a three-dimensional shape. R-group interactions help stabilize it: hydrogen bonds, ionic attractions, hydrophobic interactions, and covalent disulfide bridges between cysteines. Nonpolar side chains often cluster away from water.'],
['Quaternary: a team of chains','Some functional proteins contain two or more polypeptide subunits. Their arrangement is quaternary structure. Hemoglobin is one example. A protein made from one polypeptide does not have quaternary structure.'],
['Denaturation: losing the useful shape','Heat or extreme pH can disrupt interactions that stabilize a protein’s shape. This can change an active site or other functional region and reduce function. Typical denaturation leaves the peptide backbone and amino acid sequence intact. Some proteins can refold; others cannot under those conditions.']],
'Denaturation usually changes folding, not primary sequence. Hydrophobic interactions are not covalent bonds, and ordinary heating does not necessarily break disulfide bonds.',
'Cooking an egg changes how albumin and other proteins fold and interact. The egg white becomes opaque and firm. Cooling it does not simply turn it back into raw egg white.','protein')
add('enzymes','Enzymes & reaction rates','See why the right conditions make a difference.','↗','Unit 2 extension',[
['A catalyst you can reuse','Most enzymes are proteins. They speed reactions by lowering activation energy, the energy barrier needed to begin a reaction. Enzymes are not consumed and do not change the reaction’s overall energy difference or equilibrium.'],
['An active site with a particular fit','A substrate binds in an enzyme’s active site. The enzyme can adjust its shape slightly as binding occurs: induced fit. The active site’s shape and chemistry help determine which reactions it catalyzes. Products leave, and the enzyme can work again.'],
['Temperature has two effects','At lower temperatures molecules generally move more slowly, so reactions slow. Warming often increases rate up to a suitable range. Excessive heat can disrupt protein folding and sharply reduce activity. Different enzymes have different temperature optima.'],
['pH and concentration','Each enzyme works best over a particular pH range. Too much change can disrupt its active site. With a fixed amount of enzyme, increasing substrate raises rate until active sites are mostly occupied. Adding more substrate then has little effect.']],
'Cold temperatures usually slow an enzyme; they do not necessarily denature it. There is no one optimum temperature or pH for every enzyme.',
'To test temperature, keep enzyme amount, substrate amount, pH, and reaction time constant. Measure product formed per minute. Repeat trials to check reliability.','enzyme')
add('cells','Cells, membranes & transport','How a cell controls what enters and leaves.','◎','Beyond Unit 2',[
['A cell is an organized system','The membrane separates inside from outside. Cytoplasm holds many reactions; ribosomes build proteins. Eukaryotic cells contain a nucleus and membrane-bound organelles. Prokaryotic cells lack a nucleus but still have DNA, ribosomes, cytoplasm, and a cell membrane.'],
['Organelles work together','The nucleus holds most eukaryotic DNA. Ribosomes make proteins; rough ER and Golgi help process and route many proteins. Mitochondria carry out key steps of aerobic respiration. Plant cells also have chloroplasts, a cellulose cell wall, and usually a large central vacuole. Plants have mitochondria too.'],
['A selective boundary','A phospholipid bilayer has water-attracting heads facing outward and water-avoiding tails facing inward. Small nonpolar molecules can cross more easily than ions or large polar molecules. Transport proteins provide selective routes.'],
['Passive versus active','Diffusion is net movement down a concentration gradient. Facilitated diffusion uses a membrane protein but still moves down the gradient without direct energy input. Active transport moves substances against their gradient and requires energy. At dynamic equilibrium, particles keep moving but there is no net concentration change.'],
['Osmosis','Osmosis is net water movement across a selectively permeable membrane. When solutes cannot cross, water moves toward the side with higher effective solute concentration. A cell may swell in a hypotonic solution or shrink in a hypertonic one. A plant’s cell wall limits swelling.']],
'Diffusion is not particles deciding where to go. Random motion creates net movement down a gradient. Osmosis refers to water, not the solute.',
'A red blood cell placed in a strongly hypotonic solution takes up water and can burst. A cell wall helps protect plant cells from the same outcome.','osmosis')
add('energy','Photosynthesis & respiration','Trace matter and energy through living systems.','☀','Beyond Unit 2',[
['Photosynthesis stores energy','Plants, algae, and some bacteria use light energy to build organic molecules. A simplified overall equation is 6 CO₂ + 6 H₂O + light → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6 O₂. In plants, photosynthesis occurs in chloroplasts. Carbon in the sugar comes from CO₂, not soil.'],
['Respiration transfers energy','Cellular respiration transfers some chemical energy from food into ATP, which powers cell work. A simplified aerobic equation is glucose + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water, with energy transferred to ATP and released as heat. Glycolysis occurs in the cytoplasm; later aerobic stages involve mitochondria in eukaryotes.'],
['Plants do both','Plants photosynthesize when suitable light is available and respire day and night. Animals depend on organic molecules from food. Oxygen released by photosynthesis supports aerobic respiration, and carbon dioxide released by respiration can be used in photosynthesis.'],
['Matter cycles; energy flows','Atoms are rearranged, not created or destroyed, in these reactions. Carbon can cycle among organisms and the environment. Energy enters ecosystems, often as sunlight, and eventually disperses as heat. Energy is not recycled in the same way as matter.']],
'Plants do not get most of their mass from soil, and they do not stop respiring in the daytime. ATP transfers energy; it is not a long-term energy storage molecule like fat.',
'A growing tree gains carbon largely from CO₂ in air. Its roots supply water and minerals, and light supplies energy.','energy')
add('homeostasis','Homeostasis & body systems','How organisms keep internal conditions in range.','↔','Beyond Unit 2',[
['Stable does not mean unchanging','Homeostasis is regulation of internal conditions within a suitable range despite external changes. Temperature, blood glucose, and water balance fluctuate; feedback helps prevent harmful extremes.'],
['Negative feedback','A change is detected, a control system coordinates a response, and the response reduces the original change. When body temperature rises, sweating and increased skin blood flow help release heat. When it falls, shivering helps produce heat.'],
['Blood glucose regulation','After a meal, rising blood glucose promotes insulin release. Insulin helps many cells take up glucose and promotes storage. When blood glucose falls, glucagon promotes glucose release from the liver. These signals help keep glucose within a workable range.'],
['Systems depend on each other','Digestion supplies absorbable nutrients; respiration supplies oxygen and removes CO₂; circulation transports materials; excretion removes metabolic wastes and helps regulate water and ions. Nervous and endocrine systems coordinate responses through electrical and chemical signaling.']],
'Negative feedback means the response opposes a change; it does not mean a harmful response. Maintaining a range requires ongoing energy and coordinated systems.',
'During exercise, working muscles use more oxygen and produce more CO₂. Breathing and heart rate rise, supporting gas exchange and transport.','feedback')
add('genetics','DNA, inheritance & variation','Connect genetic information to traits.','⋈','Beyond Unit 2',[
['DNA is information in a sequence','DNA is a double-stranded polymer. A pairs with T; C pairs with G. A gene is a DNA sequence with a functional product. Chromosomes contain DNA and associated proteins. Different versions of a gene are alleles.'],
['From information to function','During transcription, RNA is made using a DNA template. During translation, ribosomes read mRNA codons to assemble amino acids into a polypeptide. Proteins help produce traits through their roles in cell structure and function. Environment can influence traits too.'],
['Cell division has different jobs','Mitosis usually makes genetically similar daughter nuclei for growth and repair. Meiosis reduces chromosome number and generates variation in gametes. Fertilization restores the diploid number by joining haploid gametes.'],
['Inheritance is probability','For a simplified single-gene cross Aa × Aa, each offspring has a 25% chance of AA, 50% Aa, and 25% aa. These probabilities apply independently to each offspring. Dominant describes expression in a heterozygote, not frequency, strength, or quality.'],
['Mutations and variation','A mutation is a change in DNA sequence. It may have no detectable effect, alter a protein, or affect gene regulation. Mutations can be harmful, helpful, or neutral depending on context. In sexual reproduction, inherited changes must be present in the cells that contribute to offspring.']],
'A dominant allele is not necessarily common. A Punnett square predicts probabilities, not an exact family outcome. Most real traits involve more than a simple one-gene model.',
'A DNA change can change an amino acid, which may change folding and function. Some DNA changes do not change the amino acid at all.','inheritance')
add('evolution','Evolution & natural selection','Explain how populations change across generations.','⤴','Beyond Unit 2',[
['Begin with variation','Individuals in a population differ. Some differences are heritable. Mutation creates new alleles; recombination reshuffles existing variation. Changes arise without organisms knowing what they will need.'],
['Selection acts on existing differences','When heritable traits affect survival and reproduction in a particular environment, individuals with those traits may leave more offspring. Over generations, those traits can become more common. Populations evolve; individual organisms do not evolve by trying.'],
['Environment changes the advantage','A trait can be advantageous in one setting and disadvantageous in another. Selection has no goal or perfect endpoint. Genetic drift can also change allele frequencies by chance, especially in small populations. Gene flow moves alleles among populations.'],
['Evidence and common ancestry','Fossils, anatomical similarities, embryological patterns, and DNA similarities provide evidence for evolution. Related organisms share inherited features because of common ancestry. Greater similarity in comparable DNA sequences often supports a more recent common ancestor.']],
'Antibiotics do not teach bacteria to resist them. Resistant variants can survive treatment and reproduce, changing the population over generations.',
'If a dark background makes pale insects easier for birds to see, inherited dark coloration may become more common—if it improves reproductive success.','selection')
add('ecology','Ecology & human impact','Follow connections between organisms and their environment.','⌘','Beyond Unit 2',[
['Levels of interaction','A population is one species in an area. A community includes interacting populations. An ecosystem includes the community and its nonliving environment. Organisms depend on factors such as food, water, temperature, and habitat.'],
['Food webs move matter and energy','Producers build organic molecules. Consumers obtain matter and energy by eating; decomposers break down dead material and return nutrients to the environment. Arrows in food webs point from the food source toward the organism receiving energy.'],
['Limits and carrying capacity','Population growth is limited by resources and environmental conditions. Carrying capacity is the population an environment can support over time; it can change when resources or conditions change. Competition, disease, predation, and disturbances can affect populations.'],
['Human actions and evidence','Habitat loss, pollution, invasive species, and climate change can alter ecosystems. Evaluate a proposed solution using evidence, tradeoffs, and measurements. Biodiversity can support ecosystem resilience, but effects depend on the ecosystem and the disturbance.']],
'Energy decreases at higher trophic levels because organisms use it and release heat. Matter is not simply lost; atoms move through organisms and the environment.',
'Fertilizer runoff can increase algal growth. When algae die, decomposition can consume dissolved oxygen, harming fish. The causal chain matters more than memorizing “fertilizer is bad.”','foodweb')
qs=[]
def q(t,p,opts,a,why):qs.append(dict(topic=t,prompt=p,options=opts,answer=a,why=why))
q('water','Which statement best explains water’s polarity?',['Oxygen attracts shared electrons more strongly than hydrogen.','Water has an overall negative charge.','Hydrogen bonds hold the O and H atoms together inside water.','Water contains ions instead of atoms.'],0,'Unequal electron sharing creates partial charges. Water is neutral overall, with polar covalent O–H bonds.')
q('water','Ice floats. How can this help a pond ecosystem in winter?',['Ice warms all water to the same temperature.','Floating ice can insulate liquid water below.','Ice supplies all the oxygen fish need.','Freezing removes all dissolved solutes.'],1,'Ice is less dense because of its open molecular arrangement. A surface layer can reduce heat loss from water below.')
q('water','Water clinging to a glass tube is an example of…',['hydrolysis.','adhesion.','denaturation.','a peptide bond.'],1,'Adhesion is attraction to another material; cohesion is attraction among water molecules.')
q('ph','Compared with pH 5, pH 3 has…',['2 times as much H⁺.','2 times less H⁺.','100 times as much H⁺.','100 times less H⁺.'],2,'Each decrease of one pH unit is a tenfold increase in H⁺. Two units means 10 × 10 = 100.')
q('ph','Why include water when testing a food with Benedict’s solution?',['It provides a negative control.','It guarantees a positive result.','It changes starch into protein.','It eliminates the need for heating.'],0,'A negative control shows what the test looks like without the target substance under the same conditions.')
q('ph','A buffer helps a cell by…',['making all solutions pH 7.','preventing any chemical reactions.','resisting changes in pH.','destroying H⁺ permanently.'],2,'Buffers accept or release H⁺ to reduce pH changes within a range; they do not force pH to 7.')
q('building','In the simplified model, joining 6 monomers into one unbranched chain releases how many waters?',['6','5','7','12'],1,'Six pieces require five links. Each link formed in the simplified dehydration model releases one water.')
q('building','A digestive enzyme breaks a polymer by adding water. This is…',['dehydration synthesis.','hydrolysis.','photosynthesis.','diffusion.'],1,'Hydrolysis uses water to split a bond. Dehydration synthesis joins building blocks and releases water.')
q('building','Which family is not a true repeating-monomer polymer?',['Proteins','DNA','Polysaccharides','Lipids'],3,'Lipids include structures such as triglycerides and steroids, rather than long repeating-monomer chains.')
q('molecules','Which pairing is correct?',['Cellulose—animal energy storage','Glycogen—animal glucose storage','Starch—genetic information','DNA—fatty acid storage'],1,'Glycogen stores glucose in animals. Starch stores glucose in plants; cellulose supports plant cell walls.')
q('molecules','A molecule contains glycerol joined to three fatty acids. It is a…',['polypeptide.','nucleotide.','triglyceride.','monosaccharide.'],2,'A triglyceride is a lipid made from glycerol and three fatty acids.')
q('molecules','Cis unsaturated fatty acid chains often make a fat more fluid because…',['they form peptide bonds.','their kinks interfere with tight packing.','they have no carbon.','they contain more DNA.'],1,'Cis double bonds introduce bends, preventing the close packing typical of straighter chains.')
q('proteins','Which protein level is the amino acid sequence?',['Primary','Secondary','Tertiary','Quaternary'],0,'Primary is sequence. Secondary is local helices/sheets, tertiary is one chain’s fold, and quaternary is multiple subunits.')
q('proteins','After typical heat denaturation, what usually remains intact?',['The original active-site shape','All hydrogen bonds','The amino acid sequence','All interactions among subunits'],2,'Typical denaturation disrupts folding while leaving most peptide bonds and thus primary sequence intact.')
q('proteins','What mainly stabilizes alpha helices and beta sheets?',['Hydrogen bonds between backbone groups','Bonds between all R groups','Glycosidic bonds','New nucleotides'],0,'Secondary structure relies on backbone C=O and N–H hydrogen bonds.')
q('proteins','Two cysteine side chains can form a…',['hydrogen ion.','disulfide bridge.','nucleotide.','starch chain.'],1,'Cysteine side chains can form a covalent disulfide bridge that stabilizes a folded protein.')
q('enzymes','An enzyme speeds a reaction by…',['raising activation energy.','lowering activation energy.','being used up.','changing all products into substrates.'],1,'The enzyme provides a lower-energy reaction pathway and can be reused.')
q('enzymes','With fixed enzyme amount, why does rate level off as substrate increases?',['Most active sites are occupied.','All enzymes turn into lipids.','Substrate destroys all water.','Activation energy becomes infinite.'],0,'At saturation, enzyme availability limits the rate; extra substrate adds little.')
q('enzymes','A student tests temperature and changes pH at the same time. Why is the conclusion weak?',['No chemical reaction can occur.','The effects of temperature and pH are confounded.','All enzymes work best at pH 7.','Temperature never affects enzymes.'],1,'Changing two independent variables makes it hard to tell which caused the result.')
q('cells','Water enters a cell when nonpenetrating solute concentration is higher inside. This is…',['osmosis.','translation.','mitosis.','dehydration.'],0,'Water moves across the membrane toward higher effective solute concentration.')
q('cells','Which transport can move a substance against its concentration gradient?',['Simple diffusion','Osmosis','Active transport','Facilitated diffusion'],2,'Active transport uses energy to move against a gradient. Facilitated diffusion is still passive.')
q('cells','Which structures are present in both typical prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells?',['Nucleus and chloroplast','DNA, ribosomes, and cell membrane','Mitochondria and Golgi','Cellulose wall and nucleus'],1,'Both have these basic structures; prokaryotes do not have a nucleus or membrane-bound organelles.')
q('energy','Most carbon in a growing tree’s organic molecules comes from…',['soil particles.','carbon dioxide in air.','sunlight atoms.','oxygen gas.'],1,'Photosynthesis incorporates carbon from CO₂. Light provides energy, not carbon atoms.')
q('energy','Which statement about plants is correct?',['They only respire at night.','They do not have mitochondria.','They respire day and night.','They obtain all energy from soil.'],2,'Plants need ATP continuously and perform respiration in light and darkness.')
q('energy','Which is recycled through ecosystems?',['Energy in a closed loop','Carbon atoms','Heat into sunlight','ATP between all organisms unchanged'],1,'Matter such as carbon cycles; energy flows through systems and disperses as heat.')
q('homeostasis','Sweating after body temperature rises is negative feedback because it…',['increases the original temperature change.','reduces the original temperature change.','makes body temperature permanently fixed.','stops all metabolism.'],1,'The response opposes the initial change and helps return temperature toward its regulated range.')
q('homeostasis','After a meal, insulin helps…',['increase blood glucose indefinitely.','promote glucose uptake and storage.','stop circulation.','turn all proteins into DNA.'],1,'Insulin signaling promotes uptake in many cells and storage, helping lower elevated blood glucose.')
q('genetics','For Aa × Aa, what is the chance of aa for each offspring?',['0%','25%','50%','100%'],1,'Each parent has a 1/2 chance of contributing a. Together: 1/2 × 1/2 = 1/4.')
q('genetics','A dominant allele must be…',['more common.','better for survival.','expressed in a heterozygote in a simple dominance model.','present in every offspring.'],2,'Dominance describes expression, not frequency, quality, or guaranteed inheritance.')
q('genetics','What process uses mRNA codons to assemble amino acids?',['Translation','Transcription','Osmosis','Replication'],0,'Ribosomes translate mRNA into a polypeptide. Transcription makes RNA from a DNA template.')
q('evolution','After antibiotic treatment, resistant bacteria become more common because…',['all bacteria choose to mutate.','resistant variants survive and leave more offspring.','antibiotics create purposeful adaptations.','individual bacteria turn into a new species immediately.'],1,'Selection favors existing or newly arising resistant variants; changes are not directed by need.')
q('evolution','What evolves through changes in inherited trait frequencies over generations?',['A single organism during its workout','A population','Only an organ','A rock'],1,'Evolution describes population changes across generations, not purposeful change within an individual.')
q('ecology','In a food web, an arrow from grass to rabbit shows…',['energy transfer from grass to rabbit.','rabbit producing grass.','the direction grass moves.','heat becoming sunlight.'],0,'Food-web arrows point from the source of food toward the recipient of its energy and matter.')
q('ecology','Why can excess fertilizer runoff lead to low dissolved oxygen?',['Fertilizer directly consumes all oxygen instantly.','Decomposition of extra dead algae uses oxygen.','Fish stop needing oxygen.','All algae stop photosynthesis forever.'],1,'Nutrients promote algal growth; decomposition of increased dead material can use dissolved oxygen.')
q('ecology','A drought reduces available water. Carrying capacity may…',['decrease because resources have changed.','stay fixed forever.','become infinite.','stop depending on resources.'],0,'Carrying capacity varies with environmental resources and conditions.')
# Each lesson provides retrieval cards from its conceptual sections, plus custom compact definitions.
cards=[]
for u in units:
 for title,body in u['parts']:cards.append(dict(topic=u['id'],front=title+' — explain it in your own words.',back=body))
terms=[('water','Cohesion','Attraction between water molecules.'),('water','Adhesion','Attraction between water and another surface.'),('ph','Buffer','A substance or system that resists pH changes by accepting or releasing H⁺.'),('building','Hydrolysis','Using water to split a covalent bond.'),('building','Dehydration synthesis','A simplified joining reaction that forms a covalent bond and releases water.'),('proteins','Primary vs. tertiary structure','Primary: amino acid order. Tertiary: the overall 3D fold of one polypeptide.'),('proteins','R group','The variable side chain of an amino acid; its properties affect folding and interactions.'),('enzymes','Activation energy','The energy barrier needed to start a reaction; enzymes lower it.'),('cells','Dynamic equilibrium','Particles continue moving, but there is no net concentration change.'),('energy','ATP','A molecule that couples energy-releasing reactions to cellular work.'),('genetics','Allele','A version of a gene.'),('evolution','Natural selection','Differential survival and reproduction associated with heritable traits in an environment.')]
for t,f,b in terms:cards.append(dict(topic=t,front=f,back=b))
open('dist/content.js','w').write('const TOPICS='+json.dumps(units)+';\nconst QUESTIONS='+json.dumps(qs)+';\nconst CARDS='+json.dumps(cards)+';\n')
